.TH C0PIN 8 "2026-09-11" "c0pin" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
c0pin \- Configure Linux CPU performance policies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c0pin
.B --performance-policy
.br
.B c0pin
.B --aggressive
.RI [ latency_us ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c0pin
is a root-only Linux utility for configuring CPU frequency and
performance policies through kernel interfaces exposed by cpufreq,
Intel pstate, and related CPU performance mechanisms.

The program supports two operating modes.

.SS Performance policy mode
When invoked with
.BR --performance-policy ,
.B c0pin
configures each available cpufreq policy to use the
.B performance
governor.

Where supported, it also sets the Energy Performance Preference (EPP)
to
.BR performance .

Performance policy mode does not modify CPU turbo or boost state,
CPU idle or C-state behavior, or CPU DMA latency QoS.

After applying and verifying the requested policy,
.B c0pin
exits.

.SS Aggressive mode
When invoked with
.BR --aggressive ,
.B c0pin
performs the performance policy configuration and additionally applies
more aggressive performance settings where the corresponding kernel
interfaces are available.

On Intel systems using Intel pstate, the program enables turbo
operation and sets the maximum performance percentage to 100 when
those controls are available.

The program also enables the kernel CPU boost mechanism where
supported.

For each cpufreq policy, the program attempts to set the maximum
frequency to the hardware-reported
.B cpuinfo_max_freq .
For drivers other than
.BR amd-pstate ,
it also attempts to set the minimum frequency to that value.
With
.BR amd-pstate ,
the minimum frequency remains controlled by the kernel driver.

Aggressive mode also opens
.B /dev/cpu_dma_latency
and writes the requested latency bound to the device.

The optional
.I latency_us
argument specifies the requested CPU DMA latency bound in
microseconds.

If
.I latency_us
is omitted, the default value is
.BR 0 .

The latency file descriptor remains open for the lifetime of the
process. The corresponding DMA latency QoS constraint is therefore
held while aggressive mode is running.

Aggressive mode waits for
.B SIGTERM
or
.B SIGINT
and exits after receiving either signal.

.SH CPU DMA LATENCY
Aggressive mode requires
.B /dev/cpu_dma_latency
to be available and accessible.

The latency lock is acquired before CPU performance state is modified.
If the latency device cannot be opened or the requested latency value
cannot be written, aggressive mode exits with an error without
applying the CPU performance configuration.

When the process exits, the latency file descriptor is closed and the
associated DMA latency QoS constraint is released.

.SH CONFIGURATION BEHAVIOR
.B c0pin
operates on the CPU policies exposed through
.BR /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq .

The exact controls available depend on the CPU, kernel version, and
active cpufreq driver.

Optional controls that are not supported by the running kernel are
skipped where appropriate.

A successful invocation therefore does not imply that every possible
CPU performance control exists or was changed.

.SH IMPORTANT
.B c0pin
does not save and restore the previous CPU configuration.

CPU settings changed by
.B c0pin
are not automatically reverted when the program exits.

In particular, stopping aggressive mode releases the
.B /dev/cpu_dma_latency
constraint, but does not automatically restore governor, EPP, boost,
turbo, or frequency-limit settings previously changed by the program.

Administrators should therefore treat mode changes as persistent
kernel configuration changes unless another tool or configuration
mechanism explicitly restores the previous state.

.SH ROOT PRIVILEGES
.B c0pin
must be executed with effective user ID 0.

The program is intended to be used as a system administration utility
or through a privileged systemd service.

.SH SYSTEMD
The project provides separate systemd services for the two operating
modes.

.TP
.B c0pin-performance.service
Runs
.B c0pin --performance-policy
as a one-shot service.

.TP
.B c0pin-aggressive.service
Runs
.B c0pin --aggressive 0
and remains active while the DMA latency constraint is held.

The two services are configured to conflict with each other and are
not intended to run simultaneously.

Stopping
.B c0pin-aggressive.service
releases the DMA latency constraint when the c0pin process exits, but
does not automatically restore other CPU settings changed by
aggressive mode.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --performance-policy
Apply the performance cpufreq governor and, where supported, the
performance EPP setting, then exit.

.TP
.B --aggressive
Apply the performance policy and additional aggressive CPU performance
settings, acquire the CPU DMA latency constraint, and remain running
until terminated.

.TP
.I latency_us
Optional CPU DMA latency bound in microseconds for aggressive mode.
The default is
.BR 0 .

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully.

Unsupported optional kernel interfaces do not by themselves cause a
failure.

.TP
.B 1
A genuine error occurred while inspecting, configuring, verifying, or
acquiring a required resource.

.SH SIGNALS
Aggressive mode handles
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
as requests for clean termination.

On termination, the CPU DMA latency file descriptor is closed,
releasing the associated DMA latency QoS constraint.

Other CPU configuration changes are not automatically reverted.

.SH FILES
.TP
.B /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/
Linux cpufreq policy interfaces.

.TP
.B /sys/devices/system/cpu/intel_pstate/
Intel pstate controls when the Intel pstate driver is available.

.TP
.B /dev/cpu_dma_latency
CPU DMA latency QoS interface used by aggressive mode.

.TP
.B /etc/systemd/system/c0pin-performance.service
Systemd service for performance policy mode.

.TP
.B /etc/systemd/system/c0pin-aggressive.service
Systemd service for aggressive mode.

.SH EXAMPLES
Apply the normal performance policy and exit:
.PP
.nf
c0pin --performance-policy
.fi

.PP
Run aggressive mode with the default zero microsecond latency bound:
.PP
.nf
c0pin --aggressive
.fi

.PP
Run aggressive mode with a 100 microsecond latency bound:
.PP
.nf
c0pin --aggressive 100
.fi

.PP
Start the performance systemd service:
.PP
.nf
systemctl start c0pin-performance.service
.fi

.PP
Start the aggressive systemd service:
.PP
.nf
systemctl start c0pin-aggressive.service
.fi

.SH NOTES
The behavior of CPU frequency and performance controls is determined
by the active Linux kernel CPU frequency driver.

In particular, Intel pstate and amd-pstate expose different controls
and may handle minimum and maximum frequency limits differently.

The presence of a cpufreq policy does not guarantee that every
attribute used by aggressive mode is available.

